  "statement": "Replacing raw floating-point calculations with behavioral audits does not eliminate regulatory capture; it simply relocates the tollbooth from counting server chips to approving proprietary test suites. When safety advocates propose replacing static thresholds with pre-deployment capability evaluations, they implicitly hand standard-setting authority over to whichever firms possess the multi-million-dollar compute clusters required to run those comprehensive red-teaming batteries. This pivot preserves the market moat under a scientific veneer, transforming external oversight into an outsourced certification process that only the largest hardware-owning conglomerates can afford to sponsor and navigate.\n\nPromising compute subsidies and safety-verified pathways for academic laboratories sounds appealing on paper, but administrative reality reveals a different dynamic for independent researchers. A grant-funded university lab cannot absorb the compliance friction of multi-layered behavioral testing or the legal liability of pre-deployment certification regimes designed for enterprise-scale operations. Subsidizing access to compute while encasing the resource inside bureaucratic gatekeeping channels merely forces academic institutions to adopt the risk frameworks and testing metrics established by commercial incumbents. The subsidy becomes a leash, tethering independent inquiry to compliance protocols that filter out unconventional methodologies before a single model is ever trained.\n\nIndependent behavioral evaluations at scale remain technically infeasible without relying on the internal telemetry, proprietary weights, and proprietary evaluation tools owned exclusively by big tech firms. When regulators outsource the work of auditing to third-party entities, those entities inevitably depend on the data infrastructure and technical talent controlled by the very corporations under review. If the verification apparatus itself requires the specialized infrastructure of dominant platforms, then oversight functions less as an independent check and more as a legitimacy-laundering service for concentrated capital. Who pays for the calibration of these evaluation benchmarks, and why should the public trust a compliance regime where the test answers are generated by the same entities writing the code?",
